window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2026-06-1", "source": "2026年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose it is proposed that university students should be required to participate in at least one research project. You are now to write a response by stating what you think of the proposal in terms of its necessity and practicality. You will have 30 minutes to write the response. You should write at least 120 words but not more than 180 words.", "sample": "Requiring university students to take part in at least one research project is a proposal worth supporting,\nprovided it is implemented flexibly and students can choose topics that interest them. From my perspective,\nsuch a policy is both necessary and practical for most undergraduates.\nFirst, it is necessary because research experience bridges the gap between textbook knowledge and real-world\nproblem solving. Through a well-designed project, students learn to ask meaningful questions, collect reliable\ndata, and think critically. These abilities are essential for their future careers. Second, it is practical because\nuniversities already have laboratories, supervisors, and course projects in place. A single semester-long project\ncan fit into the existing curriculum without placing too much burden on students.\nTherefore, I support the proposal. It helps students develop practical skills that lectures alone cannot provide,\nand its flexible design makes it achievable for most universities."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国有句老话：\"一日为师，终身为父\"。在中国人的观念里，老师不仅传授知识，更教给学生做人的道理。在古代，学生初次见老师时要举行隆重的仪式，足见老师地位之高。为弘扬尊师的优良传统，中国从1985年开始将每年的9月10日定为教师节。在这一天，学生以各种方式表达对老师的敬意。尊师是中华民族的传统美德，在当今社会得到了发扬光大。", "sample": "There is an old Chinese saying: \"A teacher for a day is a father for a lifetime.\" In the eyes of Chinese people, teachers not only impart knowledge, but also teach students how to be a good person. In ancient times, students had to hold a grand ceremony when they met their teacher for the first time, which was enough to show the high status of teachers. To carry forward the fine tradition of respecting teachers, China has observed September 10 of each year as Teachers' Day since 1985. On this day, students express their respect for teachers in various ways. Respecting teachers is a traditional virtue of the Chinese nation, and it has been carried forward in today's society."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2026-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "One of the biggest controversies over the Industrial Revolution is how it affected the working class. The oldest debates were over whether the standards of living rose or fell. Those that believed they fell claimed that the capitalists  __26__  every ounce of surplus they could from the working class. Those that believed the standards of living rose claimed that the environment was consumer-friendly, with more and more goods  __27__  to the working class every year.\nToday's historians agree that overall, the standards of living  __28__  . They do not agree, however, on when this improvement  __29__  .\nIn an effort to solve this controversy, historians have attempted to define  __30__  what \"standard of living\" means. They all seem to agree that it should mean happiness, but happiness is impossible to measure. Instead, historians  __31__  use \"real income\" as the measure for the standards of living. Real income is money  __32__  and then adjusted according to the cost of living. However, it does not take into account unemployment, pollution, health, life expectancy, or free time.\nAfter  __33__  debate and analysis of the average income, historians have agreed to disagree on whether there was rapid or slow economic growth during the Industrial Revolution. Instead, they have decided to  __34__  how the Industrial Revolution was affected by other events. They have determined that the Industrial Revolution had an overall positive  __35__  , but it quite possibly was neutralized by negative events such as wars, high taxes, and rapid population growth.", "matchIntro": "The Star Quality of New Zealand's Great Barrier Island", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "I was glad I had such terrible jetlag. Wide awake at 3:30 am with our nine-year-old daughter on our lodge balcony, I stared for hours at the most incredible night sky I've ever seen before the dawn slowly started to rub out the stars on the horizon and the multiple shooting stars faded away."}, {"label": "B", "text": "We were going to my wife's big family reunion in Auckland, New Zealand, but when we found out that Great Barrier Island, just off the coast, had been recently awarded Dark Sky Sanctuary status—a typically remote area that has an \"exceptional or distinguished quality of starry nights\"—we thought it would be the perfect place to relax before our busy schedule. It's the first island and only the fourth location in the world to achieve this status."}, {"label": "C", "text": "After a 28-hour flight with two travel-sick children, our hearts sank when we saw the tiny twin engine 10-seater that was to take us the 90km across the Hauraki Gulf to Great Barrier Island. But we needn't have worried. Whether it was the gravity force of the plane climbing or the noise of the engine, it put our three-year-old daughter instantly to sleep until we bumped down on the grass at Claris Airport. Our first empty sick bag against all the odds…Things were looking up."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Great Barrier Island has no public facilities and the 950 people living there (though only about half permanently) rely on solar and wind energy, and bottled gas for cooking. There are no streetlights. There are no cash machines or banks. The police force comprises a husband and wife, and there's one postman. It's not just the lack of light but the lack of noise that's so delightful on this unique and gorgeous scene. It was first discovered by East Polynesians about 700 years ago and the Maori name for the island is Aotea. Captain Cook gave the island its European name, because it acts as a barrier between the Pacific and the Hauraki Gulf."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The climate here is subtropical(亚热带的) like Auckland's. The island boasts a beautiful wilderness with dense forests, glorious, unspoilt sandy beaches and plenty of bays and a few mountainous areas where there are good walking tracks among the wetlands and valleys. You can birdwatch, dive, surf and boat. The best beaches are on the eastern side of the island where the surf is better, too. The sheltered western side is where you'll find the best diving and boating. Lots of people come here for the fantastic fishing spots around the island. Like a lot of spots in New Zealand, there are whale-watching trips and you might just catch sight of sharks."}, {"label": "F", "text": "It's rare to find an island that really does feel undiscovered—but this is it. When one of my wife's relations, who comes here regularly, found out that we'd been there, he whispered to us, only half-joking: \"Shh—don't tell anyone!\" The locals are a relaxed and friendly bunch—a lot of them told us that it's like what Waiheke Island (now in effect a suburb of Auckland) was in the 1970s when it was a commune of artists and people wanting to escape the city."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Our guide for some of our stay was the inestimable Hilde Hoven, a Dark Sky ambassador who runs Good Heavens with Deborah Kilgallon and Orla Cumisky. They bring their Dobsonian telescope and beanbags (it's hard work staring up wide-eyed in wonder without neck support) and, our elder daughter was glad to hear, hot chocolate to wherever you are on the island. \"It's odd but when the light readings were done,\" said Hilde, explaining how the island was awarded its special status, \"they found there was more light pollution on the northern side coming from Fiji, which is about 3,000km away, than Auckland to the south. It's pretty dark!\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "Beginning their astronomical PowerPoint demonstration using a green laser pointer, Hilde and Orla showed us the Messier 4 spherical cluster(星团) with white dwarf stars(白矮星), Saturn and its rings, the Southern Cross and the Milky Way, which stretched across the sky. Our daughter was especially taken with Hilde's narrating of the Maori myth of Māui's fish hook to explain the shape of Scorpius. In the fable, Māui hauled the North Island of New Zealand from the bottom of the ocean with his hook."}, {"label": "I", "text": "We stayed at Mount St Paul's Estate, a luxurious lodge run by the friendly Chris and Teara, both from Canada. Teara used to be a chef at the Langham in Auckland and so dinner, eaten around a huge circular table (made of one of the most ancient trees in the world), was every bit as mouth-watering as you'd expect. Eating pancakes on the balcony overlooking the unspoilt white sandy beach was a great way to start the day."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Another highlight was an hour-long walk to Kaitoke hot pools in the centre of the island, through wetlands and a forest, where we whiled away a good hour sitting in the pools shaded from the heat by layers of delicate umbrella-like plants."}, {"label": "K", "text": "We also loved visiting the potter(陶艺家) Sarah Harrison, who showed us round her studio at Shoal Bay harbour, and her many pots and jugs, plates and bowls and colourful mosaics(马赛克). In her work, she also uses a lot of materials found on the beach. Our elder daughter loved the gorgeous hidden pool at Medlands beach where the water is separated from the sea in a private rock pool. Other high points were the ancient pōhutukawa trees and the incredible views of Okiwi Basin and Whangapoua beach from the top of Windy Canyon, just a 10-minute hike through a narrow valley up some steep wooden steps on the eastern side of the island."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Apparently the island council turned down famous musician Paul McCartney's application to buy a property at Great Barrier Island because of the unwelcome attention he would have brought. You can see why he wanted to come here; equally, the island knows exactly what kind of star watching it prefers."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far.\nWhether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture.\nScience won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is \"better\". There are often trade-offs(权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration.\nOne thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides(杀虫剂)—in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products.\nIn fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests(害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never use neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide.\nTraditional agriculture has been making \"dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment.\nIt isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture.", "47": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far.\nWhether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture.\nScience won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is \"better\". There are often trade-offs(权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration.\nOne thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides(杀虫剂)—in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products.\nIn fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests(害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never use neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide.\nTraditional agriculture has been making \"dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment.\nIt isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture.", "48": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far.\nWhether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture.\nScience won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is \"better\". There are often trade-offs(权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration.\nOne thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides(杀虫剂)—in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products.\nIn fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests(害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never use neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide.\nTraditional agriculture has been making \"dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment.\nIt isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture.", "49": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far.\nWhether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture.\nScience won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is \"better\". There are often trade-offs(权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration.\nOne thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides(杀虫剂)—in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products.\nIn fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests(害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never use neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide.\nTraditional agriculture has been making \"dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment.\nIt isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture.", "50": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far.\nWhether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture.\nScience won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is \"better\". There are often trade-offs(权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration.\nOne thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides(杀虫剂)—in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products.\nIn fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests(害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never use neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide.\nTraditional agriculture has been making \"dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment.\nIt isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture.", "51": "It is still controversial whether we are fundamentally generous or selfish and whether these tendencies are shaped by our genes or environment.\nSome evidence points to humans being born cooperative. At later stages in life we routinely work together to reach goals and help out in times of need.\nYet instances of selfish behavior are also plenty. One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater good. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may be short-lived, however. Another study showed that players who cooperated did better in the long run.\nIt seems that human nature supports both pro-social and selfish traits(特征). Genetic studies have made some progress toward identifying their biological roots. By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical twins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic effects on behaviors. In these twin studies, identical and non-identical twins are asked, for example, to split a sum of money with a peer. Such studies often also rely on careful psychological assessments and DNA analysis.\nOther work highlights specific genes as key players. My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic(利他的) behavior and found that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\nAccording to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line. As communities diversified, mutual support could have broadened to include individuals not linked by blood. Another possibility is that humans cooperate to gain some advantage, such as boosting reputation. Finally, evolutionary processes take place at the group level. Groups of highly cooperative individuals have higher chances of survival because they can work together to reach goals that are unattainable to less cooperative groups.\nYet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act cooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences.", "52": "It is still controversial whether we are fundamentally generous or selfish and whether these tendencies are shaped by our genes or environment.\nSome evidence points to humans being born cooperative. At later stages in life we routinely work together to reach goals and help out in times of need.\nYet instances of selfish behavior are also plenty. One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater good. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may be short-lived, however. Another study showed that players who cooperated did better in the long run.\nIt seems that human nature supports both pro-social and selfish traits(特征). Genetic studies have made some progress toward identifying their biological roots. By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical twins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic effects on behaviors. In these twin studies, identical and non-identical twins are asked, for example, to split a sum of money with a peer. Such studies often also rely on careful psychological assessments and DNA analysis.\nOther work highlights specific genes as key players. My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic(利他的) behavior and found that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\nAccording to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line. As communities diversified, mutual support could have broadened to include individuals not linked by blood. Another possibility is that humans cooperate to gain some advantage, such as boosting reputation. Finally, evolutionary processes take place at the group level. Groups of highly cooperative individuals have higher chances of survival because they can work together to reach goals that are unattainable to less cooperative groups.\nYet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act cooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences.", "53": "It is still controversial whether we are fundamentally generous or selfish and whether these tendencies are shaped by our genes or environment.\nSome evidence points to humans being born cooperative. At later stages in life we routinely work together to reach goals and help out in times of need.\nYet instances of selfish behavior are also plenty. One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater good. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may be short-lived, however. Another study showed that players who cooperated did better in the long run.\nIt seems that human nature supports both pro-social and selfish traits(特征). Genetic studies have made some progress toward identifying their biological roots. By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical twins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic effects on behaviors. In these twin studies, identical and non-identical twins are asked, for example, to split a sum of money with a peer. Such studies often also rely on careful psychological assessments and DNA analysis.\nOther work highlights specific genes as key players. My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic(利他的) behavior and found that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\nAccording to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line. As communities diversified, mutual support could have broadened to include individuals not linked by blood. Another possibility is that humans cooperate to gain some advantage, such as boosting reputation. Finally, evolutionary processes take place at the group level. Groups of highly cooperative individuals have higher chances of survival because they can work together to reach goals that are unattainable to less cooperative groups.\nYet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act cooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences.", "54": "It is still controversial whether we are fundamentally generous or selfish and whether these tendencies are shaped by our genes or environment.\nSome evidence points to humans being born cooperative. At later stages in life we routinely work together to reach goals and help out in times of need.\nYet instances of selfish behavior are also plenty. One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater good. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may be short-lived, however. Another study showed that players who cooperated did better in the long run.\nIt seems that human nature supports both pro-social and selfish traits(特征). Genetic studies have made some progress toward identifying their biological roots. By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical twins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic effects on behaviors. In these twin studies, identical and non-identical twins are asked, for example, to split a sum of money with a peer. Such studies often also rely on careful psychological assessments and DNA analysis.\nOther work highlights specific genes as key players. My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic(利他的) behavior and found that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\nAccording to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line. As communities diversified, mutual support could have broadened to include individuals not linked by blood. Another possibility is that humans cooperate to gain some advantage, such as boosting reputation. Finally, evolutionary processes take place at the group level. Groups of highly cooperative individuals have higher chances of survival because they can work together to reach goals that are unattainable to less cooperative groups.\nYet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act cooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences.", "55": "It is still controversial whether we are fundamentally generous or selfish and whether these tendencies are shaped by our genes or environment.\nSome evidence points to humans being born cooperative. At later stages in life we routinely work together to reach goals and help out in times of need.\nYet instances of selfish behavior are also plenty. One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater good. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may be short-lived, however. Another study showed that players who cooperated did better in the long run.\nIt seems that human nature supports both pro-social and selfish traits(特征). Genetic studies have made some progress toward identifying their biological roots. By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical twins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic effects on behaviors. In these twin studies, identical and non-identical twins are asked, for example, to split a sum of money with a peer. Such studies often also rely on careful psychological assessments and DNA analysis.\nOther work highlights specific genes as key players. My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic(利他的) behavior and found that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\nAccording to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line. As communities diversified, mutual support could have broadened to include individuals not linked by blood. Another possibility is that humans cooperate to gain some advantage, such as boosting reputation. Finally, evolutionary processes take place at the group level. Groups of highly cooperative individuals have higher chances of survival because they can work together to reach goals that are unattainable to less cooperative groups.\nYet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act cooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences."}, "questions": [{"id": "2026-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "Why was Keith Stonehouse left with a $1,000 bill?", "options": ["A) He had bought meals with his online food ordering account.", "B) His son had used his cell phone to pay for playing a game.", "C) His son had ordered food from several restaurants.", "D) He had dined out at a famous five-star restaurant."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） His son had ordered food from several restaurants.\n【定位】A man was left with a $1,000 bill, after his 6-year-old son ordered a virtual buffet of food\nfrom several restaurants last weekend.\n上周末，一名男子的6岁儿子从多家餐馆点了一桌虚拟盛宴，结果他留下了一张1000美元的\n账单。\n【信号】after 因果链。新闻首句用 after his 6-year-old son ordered... 直接交代了账单产生的原\n因：after 前面是结果（$1,000 bill），后面是原因（儿子点餐）。\n【替换】his 6-year-old son ordered a virtual buffet of food from several restaurants ↔\nHis son had ordered food from several restaurants.\n概括式同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实出现了 'online food ordering account'，但用该账户点餐的是儿子，不是父亲；\nA 把动作主体从 son 偷换成 He，属于典型的主体张冠李戴。\n借用原文：his father's online food ordering account\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音提到父亲 'let his son use his cell phone to play a game'，但儿子实际上没玩游戏，\n而是去点餐了；B 把 'play a game' 偷换成 'pay for playing a game'，动作和目的都错\n了。\n借用原文：let his son use his cell phone to play a game\nD) 未提及\n录音从未提到 'five-star restaurant'，也未说父亲外出就餐；D 用高档餐厅制造生活常识诱\n惑，属于录音中找不到依据的干扰。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the news report say Keith Stonehouse is planning to do?", "options": ["A) Teach his son a lesson.", "B) Change his password.", "C) Ask his neighbors for help.", "D) Buy a bigger refrigerator."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Change his password.\n【定位】The father is planning to change his password.\n这位父亲打算更改他的密码。\n【信号】planning to 直接对应题干。is planning to change his password 中的 'planning to' 与\n题干 'is planning to do' 完全同构，后面接的 'change his password' 即为答案动作。\n【替换】change his password ↔ Change his password.\n原词复现 + 主语省略\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音中没有出现 'teach his son a lesson' 或任何惩罚、教育的表述；A 是利用家长的'常识\n反应'编造的合理但无依据选项。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到 'invited some neighbors over to eat some of it'，但目的是分享食物，不\n是 'ask his neighbors for help'；C 盗用 'neighbors' 一词并改变了动作性质。\n借用原文：invited some neighbors over to eat some of it\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音提到 'Most of the food went into the family's refrigerators'，但完全没有 'buy a\nbigger refrigerator' 的计划；D 用 'refrigerator' 这个原词做诱饵，把'已有冰箱'偷换成'要\n买更大的冰箱'。\n借用原文：refrigerators\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Animal behaviour experts looked at more than 100 research papers. They examined how visitors\naffected the behaviour of more than 250 species in zoos. They found that elephants socialised more with\neach other during public feeding times. After public feeding times, they were more likely to search for\nfood and less likely to be inactive. In the presence of many visitors, elephants were less likely to use\nrepetitive behaviours which often indicate boredom.\n动物行为专家查阅了100多篇研究论文。他们研究了参观者如何影响动物园中250多种动物的行为。他们\n发现，在面向公众的喂食时段，大象彼此之间的社交活动更多。公众喂食时段结束后，大象更有可能寻找\n食物，也不太可能处于不活动状态。在众多参观者在场时，大象较少表现出重复性行为，而这类行为通常\n表明它们感到无聊。\n2 Visitors had a similar effect on some other species too, including polar bears, certain jungle cats and\nblack-tailed plain dogs. Some animal species have been born and raised in zoos, and so have likely\nbecome used to the presence of humans. Zoo visitors are often aspects of a zoo animal's environment\nthat animals cannot control, and as such can be stressful, although some species appear to show good\nadaptability to the changing conditions of visitors.\n参观者对其他一些物种也产生了类似影响，包括北极熊、某些丛林猫科动物和黑尾草原犬。一些动物物种\n出生并成长于动物园，因此很可能已经习惯了人类的存在。对动物园里的动物而言，参观者通常是其环境\n中动物无法控制的因素，因此可能会造成压力，尽管有些物种似乎表现出对参观者状况变化的良好适应\n能力。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What did animal behaviour experts do according to the news report?", "options": ["A) They examined how visitors affected animal behaviour in zoos.", "B) They studied when the best time to feed animals in public was.", "C) They studied how animals adapted themselves to life in zoos.", "D) They examined what behaviours indicated animal boredom."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They examined how visitors affected animal behaviour in zoos.\n【定位】They examined how visitors affected the behaviour of more than 250 species in zoos.\n他们研究了游客如何影响动物园里250多个物种的行为。\n【信号】直接陈述研究内容的动作句。Paragraph 1 开头先说专家 looked at more than 100\nresearch papers，紧接着 They examined how visitors affected the behaviour of more\nthan 250 species in zoos. 用 examined 直接回答题干；对新闻细节题，这种紧跟主题引\n入的动作句就是答案落点。\n【替换】They examined how visitors affected the behaviour of more than 250 species in zoos. ↔\nThey examined how visitors affected animal behaviour in zoos.\n具体数量概括为 animal，研究对象一致\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音提到 during public feeding times 与 After public feeding times，但这是在说大象\n的行为变化，B 项把 public feeding times 改成 'the best time to feed animals'，既偷换\n概念又答非所问。\n借用原文：public feeding times\nC) 偷换概念\nsome species appear to show good adaptability to the changing conditions of\nvisitors 讲的是适应游客变化，C 项改成 'adapted themselves to life in zoos'，对象从\nvisitors 变成 zoos，属于偷换概念。\n借用原文：good adaptability to the changing conditions of visitors\nD) 原词陷阱\nrepetitive behaviours which often indicate boredom 只是研究中的一个发现细节，D 项\n把它上升为专家的研究主题 'what behaviours indicated animal boredom'。\n借用原文：repetitive behaviours which often indicate boredom", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "Why have some animal species likely become used to the presence of humans?", "options": ["A) They socialise with zookeepers as well as with each other.", "B) They show good adaptability to living with humans.", "C) They have been kept as pets by humans.", "D) They have been born and raised in zoos."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They have been born and raised in zoos.\n【定位】Some animal species have been born and raised in zoos, and so have likely become used to\nthe presence of humans.\n有些动物物种在动物园出生、长大，因此可能已经习惯了人类的存在。\n【信号】因果连词 and so 把原因和结果钉在一起。题干中的 'become used to the presence of\nhumans' 与答案句后半句完全对应，前半句 'have been born and raised in zoos' 由 and\nso 引出，正是问题要的 why。\n【替换】have been born and raised in zoos ↔ They have been born and raised in zoos.\n原因部分被原样截取，主语由 Some animal species 换成 They\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nelephants socialised more with each other 是 Paragraph 1 中关于大象的发现，与题干\n问的 'some animal species' 习惯人类无关，且 zookeepers 在录音中从未出现。\n借用原文：socialised more with each other\nB) 答非所问\n录音确实提到 some species appear to show good adaptability to the changing\nconditions of visitors，但这是习惯人类之后表现出的能力，是结果；题干问 why，B 项答\n非所问。\n借用原文：good adaptability to the changing conditions of visitors\nC) 未提及 录音只说 born and raised in zoos 导致习惯人类存在，从未提到 as pets。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What is Denver doing with its popular electric bike incentive program?", "options": ["A) Adjusting it.", "B) Suspending it.", "C) Reevaluating it.", "D) Expanding it."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Suspending it.\n【定位】Denver is suspending its popular electric bike incentive program.\n丹佛正在暂停其受欢迎的电动自行车激励项目。\n【信号】新闻首句直接点题。Denver is suspending its popular electric bike incentive program\n位于新闻首句，直接回答了 What is Denver doing 的问题；suspending 与选项 B 的核心\n动词完全一致。\n【替换】is suspending ↔ Suspending it\n现在进行时动作直接对应\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n选项 A 的 Adjusting 看似与原文 plans design changes 沾边，但 design changes 是\nplan（计划），不是当前正在做的动作，且题干问的是 doing（现在进行）。\n借用原文：plans design changes to make participation easier\nC) 未提及 选项 C 的 Reevaluating 在录音中没有对应表述，属于凭空捏造。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 D 的 Expanding it 容易被 increase access to the program 诱惑，但 increase\naccess 是 plans（未来设计目标），且项目当前是暂停而非扩张。\n借用原文：increase access to the program", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "How did the electric bike incentive program benefit residents?", "options": ["A) They could buy an electric bike for $400 in April.", "B) They could buy their first electric bike at a discount.", "C) They could enjoy free after-sale service for their purchase.", "D) They could get $1,200 from government funds if they qualified."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They could get $1,200 from government funds if they qualified.\n【定位】It allowed Denver residents to apply for $400 in government funds before buying an electric\nbike from a participating shop. Residents who met certain income qualifications could qualify\nfor $1,200.\n它允许丹佛居民在从参与商店购买电动自行车之前申请400美元的政府资金。符合特定收入\n资格的居民可以获得1,200美元。\n【信号】居民获益的具体说明。题干 benefit residents 问居民如何受益，原文 allowed Denver\nresidents to apply for $400 和 Residents who met certain income qualifications\ncould qualify for $1,200 正是对居民所获资助的直接说明。\n【替换】Residents who met certain income qualifications could qualify for $1,200 ↔\nThey could get $1,200 from government funds if they qualified\n同义替换：资格条件与获得资助的改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 A 把 $400 in government funds 和 launched in April 两个原词拼凑成 buy an\nelectric bike for $400 in April，但原文说的是申请 $400 资金，不是花 $400 买车；April\n是项目启动时间，不是买车时间。\n借用原文：$400 in government funds before buying an electric bike; launched in April\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 B 盗用原文 buying an electric bike，但原文说的是申请政府补贴，不是直接打折；\ndiscount 在录音中并未出现。\n借用原文：buying an electric bike from a participating shop\nC) 未提及 选项 C 的 after-sale service 在原文中没有对应内容，属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the city of Denver expect to do early next year?", "options": ["A) Put 400 more electric bikes on its streets.", "B) Increase the program funding by 8 percent.", "C) Resume its electric bike incentive program.", "D) Invest millions of dollars into the local economy."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Resume its electric bike incentive program.\n【定位】The city expects to resume the program early next year.\n该市预计将于明年初恢复该项目。\n【信号】题干关键词直接复现。原文 expects to resume the program early next year 与题干\nexpect to do early next year 的关键词 expects / early next year 直接对应，resume 就\n是答案动作。\n【替换】expects to resume the program early next year ↔ Resume its electric bike incentive program\n同义替换：resume the program = Resume its electric bike incentive program\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 A 把 $400 和 put 4,400 electric bikes on the streets 拼凑成 Put 400 more electric\nbikes on its streets，数字和时态都错了。\n借用原文：$400 in government funds; put 4,400 electric bikes on the streets of Denver\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 B 的 8 percent 在原文中没有依据，可能是把 68% of the program funding 误听或\n篡改而来。\n借用原文：68% of the program funding\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 D 盗用原文 invested $8.6 million into the local economy，但原文用的是 invested\n（已完成），而题干问的是 expect to do early next year（未来计划），时态不符。\n借用原文：invested $8.6 million into the local economy", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What exhibits does the man like best in the museum he visited?", "options": ["A) The coin collection.", "B) The primitive tools.", "C) The ancient ornaments.", "D) The 2,000-year-old clothes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The coin collection.\n【定位】The primitive tools early humans used tell a lot about their life, but my favorite is the\ncoin collection.\n早期人类使用的原始工具能告诉我们很多关于他们生活的信息，但我最喜欢的是硬币收藏。\n【信号】转折 but + favorite 点出最爱。男士先提 The primitive tools，随即用 but my favorite\nis 把重点转向 coin collection，favorite 与题干 like best 同义。\n【替换】my favorite is the coin collection ↔ The coin collection.\nfavorite = like best\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThe primitive tools. 确实出现在 The primitive tools early humans used...，但它是 but\n之前的铺垫，与 like best 无关。\n借用原文：The primitive tools early humans used tell a lot about their life\nC) 未提及 录音中只提到 tools 与 coin collection，完全没有 ornaments 这一展品。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe 2,000-year-old clothes. 盗用了 over 2,000 years old 这一数字，但原文说的是\ncoins，不是 clothes。\n借用原文：some coins that are over 2,000 years old", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the woman say about the old city centre?", "options": ["A) It is charming with narrow but beautiful streets.", "B) It attracts visitors from every corner of the world.", "C) It has flowers displayed around the Trevi Fountain.", "D) It is more attractive than the pictures in guidebooks."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is charming with narrow but beautiful streets.\n【定位】The streets are narrow but beautiful with plenty of charm.\n街道狭窄但很美，充满了魅力。\n【信号】直接描述 + but 强调 beautiful/charm。女士用 narrow but beautiful with plenty of\ncharm 直接描述 old city centre 的街道，but 后的 beautiful 与 charm 是评价重心。\n【替换】The streets are narrow but beautiful with plenty of charm ↔\nIt is charming with narrow but beautiful streets.\ncharm→charming，同词保留 narrow but beautiful\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nIt attracts visitors from every corner of the world. 盗用 it's like the whole world is\nhere on holiday，但这是对整个城市旺季的感受，不是 old city centre 本身。\n借用原文：it's like the whole world is here on holiday\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt has flowers displayed around the Trevi Fountain. 盗用 with flowers，但 flowers 是\n在 Spanish steps 的台阶上，不是 Trevi Fountain 周围。\n借用原文：with flowers that go up to a church\nD) 过度推断\nIt is more attractive than the pictures in guidebooks. 中的 guidebooks 出现在 in all\nthe guidebooks，但女士只说 Trevi Fountain 上了 guidebooks，并未比较吸引力。\n借用原文：in all the guidebooks", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the man say about the square with the Spanish steps?", "options": ["A) It is covered with wild grass.", "B) It is very famous and pretty.", "C) It is located away from an old church.", "D) It is surrounded by Spanish-style houses."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is very famous and pretty.\n【定位】It's a square where on one side, there are some very wide steps with flowers that go up to a\nchurch. It's also in all the tourist guidebooks. It's very famous and pretty.\n这是一个广场，其中一侧有非常宽阔、饰满鲜花的台阶通向一座教堂。它也在所有旅游指南\n里。它非常有名而且漂亮。\n【信号】连续介绍后 famous/pretty 给出评价。男士先说明 square 的物理特征，再用 It's very\nfamous and pretty. 给出总体评价，这是问题的直接回答。\n【替换】It's very famous and pretty. ↔ It is very famous and pretty.\nIt's → It is 缩写展开，形容词原词保留\n【排除】A) 未提及 录音提到 wide steps with flowers，完全没有 wild grass。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt is located away from an old church. 与 steps with flowers that go up to a church 相\n反。\n借用原文：steps with flowers that go up to a church\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt is surrounded by Spanish-style houses. 盗用 Spanish 一词，但原文只说 square with\nthe Spanish steps，未提 surrounding houses。\n借用原文：visiting the square with the Spanish steps", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the woman say she is going to do first?", "options": ["A) Ask Kathy what her plan is.", "B) Tell Kathy Mike will join them.", "C) Ask Kathy if she'd like to see the square.", "D) Tell Kathy Mike would like to have a change."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Ask Kathy if she'd like to see the square.\n【定位】Let me ask her first, but I'm sure she'll be up for it.\n让我先问问她，但我相信她会愿意的。\n【信号】first 直接对应题干 first。女士用 Let me ask her first 明确动作的先后顺序，first 与题干\nfirst 同词对应。\n【替换】Let me ask her first ↔ Ask Kathy if she'd like to see the square.\nask her → Ask Kathy；语境指约她看 square\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nAsk Kathy what her plan is. 把 ask her 曲解为询问计划，但前文已计划去看 square，实\n际问的是愿不愿意加入。\nB) 偷换概念\nTell Kathy Mike will join them. 把 ask 偷换成 tell，且 Kathy 是否加入尚未确定，不能提\n前告知。\nD) 张冠李戴\nTell Kathy Mike would like to have a change. 盗用 Doing tourist stuff with others\nwould be a welcome change，但这是 Mike 的想法，不是女士要 first 做的事。\n借用原文：Doing tourist stuff with others would be a welcome change", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What advice does the man want to get from the woman?", "options": ["A) How to fit into the bigger picture.", "B) How to get on with colleagues.", "C) How to create a solid first impression.", "D) How to succeed in a new job."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） How to succeed in a new job.\n【定位】Can you give me advice about being successful at a new job? I start mine next week.\n你能给我一些关于在新工作中取得成功的建议吗？我下周开始新工作。\n【信号】开门见山的请求句。对话首句即以 Can you give me advice about… 直接提出求助，后接\nI start mine next week 点明新工作背景，是整段对话的总起与问题来源。\n【替换】being successful at a new job ↔ How to succeed in a new job.\n同义转写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHow to fit into the bigger picture. 中的 bigger picture 确实在 P10 出现，但它是女士后\n续建议的内容，不是男士最初想获得的建议。\n借用原文：fits into the bigger picture\nB) 原词陷阱\nHow to get on with colleagues. 中的 colleagues 相关概念在 P3 出现，但那是男士表示\n意识到同事重要性，并非他向女士寻求的核心建议。\n借用原文：collaborate with my coworkers\nC) 张冠李戴\nHow to create a solid first impression. 几乎原样出现在 P2，但那是女士给的第一条建\n议，不是男士主动求的建议。\n借用原文：create a solid first impression", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the woman say is the most important thing for the man to do at first?", "options": ["A) Get familiar with all the details of his job description.", "B) Learn about the relations within the organization.", "C) Recognize his own limitations and weaknesses.", "D) Understand his role from various perspectives."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Understand his role from various perspectives.\n【定位】The most important thing for you to do at first is to understand your role from\nmultiple perspectives.\n你一开始要做的最重要的事，就是从多个角度理解你的角色。\n【信号】最高级 + 建议句。The most important thing…is to… 是典型的答案信号，直接给出女士\n认为的最优先行动。\n【替换】understand your role from multiple perspectives ↔\nUnderstand his role from various perspectives.\n同义词替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nGet familiar with all the details of his job description. 中的 job description 在 P6 原词\n出现，但录音说的是 what you do is not just limited to your job description，即「不只\n限于岗位描述」，恰恰否定了此项。\n借用原文：job description\nB) 偷换概念\nLearn about the relations within the organization. 对应 how your work relates to\nother people within the organization，但那是解释「为什么要多角度理解角色」的原\n因，不是 the most important thing 本身。\n借用原文：how your work relates to other people within the organization\nC) 未提及\n录音中没有任何地方提到 limitations and weaknesses 或让男士认识自身弱点。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man say he is feeling a bit uneasy about?", "options": ["A) Learning history.", "B) Meeting his boss.", "C) Creating reports for other departments to use.", "D) Leaving his coworkers in the present company."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Meeting his boss.\n【定位】I'm feeling a bit uneasy about meeting my boss as well.\n我对去见我的老板也感到有点不安。\n【信号】情感表达 + about 对象。feeling a bit uneasy about meeting my boss 直接陈述男士的\n情绪及其对象。\n【替换】meeting my boss ↔ Meeting his boss.\n原词复现，人称转换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nLearning history. 中的 history 在 P9 出现，但那是男士举例「了解公司历史」，并非他感\n到不安的对象。\n借用原文：history of the company\nC) 原词陷阱\nCreating reports for other departments to use. 中的 reports 和 department 在 P7 出\n现，但那是男士用来举例说明工作关联性，与不安情绪无关。\n借用原文：reports that I create at the end of the month may be used by a team in\nanother department\nD) 与原文相反\nLeaving his coworkers in the present company. 中的 leaving 与录音中 collaborate\nwith my coworkers 的「合作」态度相反，且录音未提及任何离职/离开含义。\n借用原文：collaborate with my coworkers", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the woman suggest the man do to gain insight about the running of the business?", "options": ["A) Get to know the decision-makers.", "B) Acquire decision-making authority.", "C) Promote its products and services.", "D) Observe its overall management."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Get to know the decision-makers.\n【定位】However, it's crucial that you get to know the people who have decision-making authority to\ngain insight regarding how the business is run and decisions are made.\n然而，你必须去认识那些有决策权的人，以便了解企业是如何运作、决策是如何做出的。\n【信号】建议句 + 目的状语。it's crucial that you get to know…to gain insight regarding… 中，\n主句给出建议动作，不定式给出目的，正好对应题干 to gain insight about the running\nof the business。\n【替换】get to know the people who have decision-making authority ↔ Get to know the decision-makers.\n名词化同义替换\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nAcquire decision-making authority. 盗用 decision-making authority 原词，但原文说的\n是「认识拥有决策权的人」，而非男士自己去「获取」决策权。\n借用原文：people who have decision-making authority\nC) 原词陷阱\nPromote its products and services. 中的 products and services 在 P10 出现，但那是\n「了解公司业务」的内容，不是「了解企业运作与决策」的建议。\n借用原文：products and services that the company exists to deliver\nD) 过度推断\nObserve its overall management. 看似与 how the business is run 相关，但原文具体建\n议是「认识决策者」，而非泛泛地「观察整体管理」。\n借用原文：how the business is run and decisions are made", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does research indicate about many consumers when they evaluate a purchase?", "options": ["A) They do not tell marketers their feeling about a product.", "B) They do not compare competing brands and prices.", "C) They are not hesitant to contradict themselves.", "D) They are not sure what they really like to buy."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They do not compare competing brands and prices.\n【定位】Many consumers report comparing multiple competing brands and prices when evaluating a\npurchase. However, research indicates that this is not actually the case.\n许多消费者声称自己在评估购买时会比较多个竞争品牌和价格。然而，研究表明事实并非\n如此。\n【信号】转折 however 否定前文自我报告。However, research indicates that this is not actually\nthe case. 中的 however 把前一句消费者自我报告的内容推翻，research indicates 后的否\n定才是答案所在。\n【替换】research indicates that this is not actually the case ↔\nThey do not compare competing brands and prices.\n否定前文的同义改写\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n原文讨论的是消费者真实想法与所说之间的矛盾，并非'是否告诉营销人员感受'，选项把话\n题偷换成 marketers。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey are not hesitant to contradict themselves. 盗用了原文 What consumers really\nthink or feel often contradicts what they say. 中的 contradict，但原文是'所想/所感与所\n说矛盾'，并非'毫不犹豫地自相矛盾'。\n借用原文：What consumers really think or feel often contradicts what they say.\nD) 未提及\n原文只说消费者被 unconscious urges / emotion 驱动，并未说他们'不确定自己喜欢买什\n么'，属于根据常识编造的选项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do good marketers keep in mind all the time?", "options": ["A) Consumers are often attracted by new products.", "B) Consumers tend to make decisions on the spot.", "C) Consumers find it hard to resist temptation.", "D) Consumers are actually driven by emotion."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Consumers are actually driven by emotion.\n【定位】Emotion is what really drives the purchasing behaviors, and also, decision making in general.\nGood marketers keep in mind this important fact all the time, and examples of emotion-based\ncampaigns are everywhere.\n情感才是真正驱动购买行为、乃至一般决策的东西。优秀的营销人员始终牢记这一重要事\n实，基于情感的营销活动例子比比皆是。\n【信号】指代 this important fact 回指前句主旨。Good marketers keep in mind this\nimportant fact all the time 中的 this important fact 明确回指上一句 Emotion is what\nreally drives the purchasing behaviors, and also, decision making in general.，答案就\n在前一句。\n【替换】Emotion is what really drives the purchasing behaviors, and also, decision making in general. ↔\nConsumers are actually driven by emotion.\n主动变被动，drive 同根转换\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文提到 marketing campaigns 和各类产品，但从未说消费者'被新产品吸引'，属于无中\n生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\nConsumers tend to make decisions on the spot. 盗用了原文 decision making in\ngeneral 中的 decision，但原文讲的是'决策一般被情感驱动'，不是'当场做决定'。\n借用原文：decision making in general\nC) 未提及\n原文讲 emotion / unconscious urges 驱动消费者，但并未出现 temptation 或'难以抗拒\n诱惑'的表述。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What should marketers do besides stressing the features of a product?", "options": ["A) Draw consumers' attention to the luxury goods symbolizing quality life.", "B) Leave consumers the impression they are doing nothing manipulative.", "C) Highlight the emotional response it will trigger in the consumer.", "D) Remind consumers of the wide connections it can help to build."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Highlight the emotional response it will trigger in the consumer.\n【定位】As marketers, we should focus on the features of the product, but we must also sell the\nlifestyle and the feeling. The key is to highlight the emotional response a consumer will have\nby using the product.\n作为营销人员，我们应该关注产品特性，但也必须推销生活方式和感觉。关键是突出消费者\n使用产品后产生的情感反应。\n【信号】but / also 补充 + The key is to 强调。but we must also sell the lifestyle and the\nfeeling 先补充应做之事，随后 The key is to highlight the emotional response... 用 The\nkey is to 进一步强调核心动作，答案落在这里。\n【替换】highlight the emotional response a consumer will have by using the product ↔\nHighlight the emotional response it will trigger in the consumer.\n同义替换：have by using → trigger in\n【排除】A)\nDraw consumers' attention to the luxury goods symbolizing quality life. 盗用原文\nLuxury goods target our feelings of self-worth, acceptance, and status in the world.\n中的 luxury goods，但该句只是论证情感营销的例子，不是题干问的'除了 features 还应该\n做什么'。\n借用原文：Luxury goods target our feelings of self-worth, acceptance, and status in the world.\nB) 原词陷阱\nLeave consumers the impression they are doing nothing manipulative. 盗用原文\nThere's nothing manipulative about helping consumers achieve desired states of\nemotion. 中的 manipulative，但原文是在为情感营销辩护，不是把它作为营销人员'应该做\n的事'。\n借用原文：There's nothing manipulative about helping consumers achieve desired states\nof emotion.\nD)\nRemind consumers of the wide connections it can help to build. 盗用原文\nCommunication devices excite us by offering a connection to friends, family and a\nbroader network of people. 中的 connection / broader network，但这只是\ncommunication devices 的例子，不是营销人员的总体做法。\n借用原文：Communication devices excite us by offering a connection to friends, family and a\nbroader network of people.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the passage say about Americans?", "options": ["A) They are taking in a lot of plastic.", "B) They are polluting the environment.", "C) They consume much processed food.", "D) They make everything with plastics."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are taking in a lot of plastic.\n【定位】Americans are consuming and breathing in a lot of plastic.\n美国人正在摄入并吸入大量塑料。\n【信号】段首总领句。Americans are consuming and breathing in a lot of plastic. 是第二段首\n句，开门见山给出美国人摄入塑料的结论。\n【替换】Americans are consuming and breathing in a lot of plastic ↔ taking in a lot of plastic\n同义概括\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文 they escaped the confines of packaging and objects and settled in the\nenvironment 说的是塑料 escape 到环境中，而不是美国人正在污染环境。\n借用原文：they escaped the confines of packaging and objects and settled in the environment\nC) 未提及\n短文只提到 food we eat、the average American diet，没有说美国人 consume much\nprocessed food。\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文 Plastics have for years been used to make nearly everything that surrounds us\n的主语是 plastics，而不是 Americans。\n借用原文：Plastics have for years been used to make nearly everything that surrounds us", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What counted as the biggest sources for Americans breathing in or consuming microplastics?", "options": ["A) Beer, salts and added sugars.", "B) Tap water and take-away food.", "C) Food packages and plastic bags.", "D) Air, bottled water and seafood."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Air, bottled water and seafood.\n【定位】Data shows that for Americans, air, bottled water and seafood were the biggest sources for\nbreathed-in or consumed microplastics.\n数据显示，对美国人而言，空气、瓶装水和海鲜是吸入或摄入微塑料的最大来源。\n【信号】Data shows＋最高级 biggest。Data shows that for Americans, air, bottled water and\nseafood were the biggest sources for breathed-in or consumed microplastics. 中\nData shows 引出结论，biggest sources 直接对应题干的 biggest sources。\n【替换】air, bottled water and seafood were the biggest sources for breathed-in or consumed microplastics\n↔ Air, bottled water and seafood\n原词复现\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n原文 Added sugars accounted for less, and salts, tap water and beer contributed\nminimal amounts 说明啤酒、盐、添加糖所占比例更少或极小，不是 biggest sources。\n借用原文：Added sugars accounted for less, and salts, tap water and beer contributed\nminimal amounts\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 salts, tap water and beer contributed minimal amounts 说明 tap water 是极小\n量，不是最大来源；take-away food 则根本没出现。\n借用原文：salts, tap water and beer contributed minimal amounts\nC) 未提及\nFood packages and plastic bags 听起来像塑料来源，但原文说的是 larger plastic\nobjects fragment into smaller pieces，并未把它们列为最大来源。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the passage say is still unknown?", "options": ["A) How much plastic there is in the remaining 85% of Americans' typical diet.", "B) What measures can be taken to prevent plastic objects from fragmenting.", "C) What harm microplastics may actually do to the average Americans.", "D) How many ways there are for microplastics to get into food and air."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） How much plastic there is in the remaining 85% of Americans' typical diet.\n【定位】It's not yet known how much plastic is contained in the remaining 85% of the typical diet.\n典型饮食中剩余 85% 含有多少塑料尚不清楚。\n【信号】not yet known 直接对应 unknown。It's not yet known how much plastic is\ncontained in the remaining 85% of the typical diet. 中的 not yet known 与题干 still\nunknown 同义。\n【替换】how much plastic is contained in the remaining 85% of the typical diet ↔\nHow much plastic there is in the remaining 85% of Americans' typical diet\n同义改写\n【排除】B) 未提及\n原文 Many start off as part of larger plastic objects, which over time fragment into\nsmaller and smaller pieces until they become tiny 只讲碎片化过程，没有讲如何\nprevent。\n借用原文：Many start off as part of larger plastic objects, which over time fragment into smaller\nand smaller pieces until they become tiny\nC) 未提及 短文只谈了微塑料的摄入量和来源，没有讲 harm 或实际危害。\nD) 答非所问\n原文 Microplastics get into food and air in several ways 已经说明有几种途径，因此“有\n多少种方式”不是 still unknown。\n借用原文：Microplastics get into food and air in several ways", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What did over half of the respondents say in the survey?", "options": ["A) They planned to adopt a pet animal.", "B) They kept not only a dog but a cat.", "C) They had at least one pet.", "D) They liked cats more than dogs."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They had at least one pet.\n【定位】55% said they owned at least one pet.\n55%的人说他们至少养了一只宠物。\n【信号】数字比例直接作答。题干问『超过半数受访者说了什么』，而55% said they owned at\nleast one pet中55%正好对应over half，数字句就是答案句。\n【替换】55% ↔ over half\n数字同义替换\nowned at least one pet ↔ had at least one pet\n同义词替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey planned to adopt a pet animal盗用了adopt a pet animal原词，但原文主语是\nPeople looking to stay well as they age（希望保持健康的老年人），且说的是may want\nto（可能想），并非调查中过半受访者的实际行为。\n借用原文：People looking to stay well as they age may want to adopt a pet animal\nB) 偷换概念\nThey kept not only a dog but a cat把原文Dogs were the most common pet, followed\nby cats曲解为『同时养狗和猫』，但原文只说明狗最常见、猫次之，并未说同一人同时养两\n种。\n借用原文：Dogs were the most common pet, followed by cats\nD) 与原文相反\nThey liked cats more than dogs与原文Dogs were the most common pet, followed by\ncats相反，原文表明狗比猫更常见，即人们更常养狗而非更喜欢猫。\n借用原文：Dogs were the most common pet, followed by cats", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did most older pet owners in the survey say their pets provided?", "options": ["A) A sense of pride.", "B) A sense of duty.", "C) A sense of purpose.", "D) A sense of security."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A sense of purpose.\n【定位】Nearly 90% of older pet owners said their animals helped them enjoy life and feel loved;\nroughly 80% said their pets reduced stress; and almost three-quarters said their pets provided\na sense of purpose.\n近90%的老年宠物主人表示，他们的动物帮助他们享受生活并感到被爱；约80%的人说宠物\n减轻了他们的压力；近四分之三的人说宠物给了他们一种目标感。\n【信号】排比列举中的第三项。原文Nearly 90%...; roughly 80%...; and almost three-quarters\nsaid their pets provided a sense of purpose用排比列举宠物带来的三种好处，题干问\n『most』对应的是almost three-quarters（近四分之三）这一组，其后的provided a\nsense of purpose即为答案。\n【替换】almost three-quarters ↔ most\n比例同义替换\ntheir pets provided a sense of purpose ↔ A sense of purpose\n原词复现\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA sense of pride中的pride在原文P2的排比列举中并未出现，原文只提到enjoy life、feel\nloved、reduced stress和a sense of purpose。\nB) 未提及\nA sense of duty中的duty在原文P2中未出现；虽然照顾宠物可能隐含责任，但原文并未把\n它作为宠物『提供』给主人的感受。\nD) 未提及\nA sense of security中的security在原文P2中未出现，原文只提到feel loved（感到被爱）\n和reduced stress（减轻压力），并未涉及安全感。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the passage say people did report in the survey?", "options": ["A) A few problems with keeping big animals.", "B) Some disadvantages related to pet ownership.", "C) A few cases of getting attacked by pet animals.", "D) Some conflicts with neighbors due to pet keeping."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Some disadvantages related to pet ownership.\n【定位】However, people did report a few disadvantages to pet ownership.\n然而，人们确实报告了一些养宠物的缺点。\n【信号】转折词however引出负面信息。前文P2讲宠物带来的种种好处，随后用However一转，引\n出people did report a few disadvantages to pet ownership，正好对应题干所问。\n【替换】a few disadvantages to pet ownership ↔ Some disadvantages related to pet ownership\n同义替换+限定词替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA few problems with keeping big animals中的big animals在原文P2中并未出现，原文\n只泛泛讨论pet ownership，未限定动物体型。\nC) 偷换概念\nA few cases of getting attacked by pet animals把原文6% of pet owners said they'd\nfallen or injured themselves as a result of having a pet中的injured偷换为attacked by\npet animals，受伤不等于被动物攻击。\n借用原文：6% of pet owners said they'd fallen or injured themselves as a result of having a pet\nD) 未提及\nSome conflicts with neighbors due to pet keeping中的neighbors和conflicts在原文P2\n中均未提及，原文提到的缺点是difficulty traveling和financial strains。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does research show interacting with pets can do?", "options": ["A) Reduce as many symptoms of depression as medical help.", "B) Improve people's health as much as physical activities.", "C) Lower older people's stress as effectively as exercising.", "D) Bring many of the same benefits as human social support."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Bring many of the same benefits as human social support.\n【定位】Human social support comes with a host of health benefits—less stress, lower rates of long-\nterm disease and more—and research shows that interacting with pets can bring many of the\nsame benefits.\n人类的社会支持带来诸多健康益处——压力更小、长期疾病发病率更低等等——研究表明，\n与宠物互动也能带来许多同样的益处。\n【信号】研究结论句直接作答。题干问research show interacting with pets can do，原文\nresearch shows that interacting with pets can bring many of the same benefits直接\n给出研究结论，是答案句。\n【替换】interacting with pets can bring many of the same benefits ↔\nBring many of the same benefits as human social support\n原句概括+比较对象补全\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nReduce as many symptoms of depression as medical help盗用了原文reduce\nsymptoms of depression原词，但额外添加了as...as medical help这一原文没有的医疗帮\n助比较，属于强加了不存在的关系。\n借用原文：animals can lower stress, reduce symptoms of depression and anxiety and encourage\nphysical activity\nB) 偷换概念\nImprove people's health as much as physical activities把原文encourage physical\nactivity（鼓励身体活动）偷换成『与身体活动效果一样好』，并添加了as much as比较，\n原文并未将养宠物与physical activities作效果比较。\n借用原文：animals can lower stress, reduce symptoms of depression and anxiety and encourage\nphysical activity\nC) 偷换概念\nLower older people's stress as effectively as exercising把原文animals can lower stress\n和encourage physical activity两个独立好处拼接成『降低压力的效果与锻炼一样好』，但\n原文并未将lower stress与exercising进行效果比较。\n借用原文：animals can lower stress, reduce symptoms of depression and anxiety and encourage\nphysical activity", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) squeezed\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 the capitalists 后缺谓语，宾语从句用过去时 → 过去式及物动词\n【依据】squeeze...from... 压榨搭配，贴合\"生活水平下降\"一方的控诉\nsqueezed 填入得 the capitalists squeezed every ounce of surplus they could from\nthe working class（资本家从工人阶级身上榨取每一盎司剩余）——squeeze...from...\n是\"从……中榨取\"的固定搭配，空后的 from 正是它的信号。更关键的是语境：本句是\"认为\n生活水平下降\"一方的观点，Those that believed they fell claimed that...，控诉资本家压\n榨，squeezed 的负面感情色彩与控诉口吻完全一致。\n【竞争词】C) earned 逻辑指向反：剩余价值是\"榨\"来的，不是资本家\"挣\"来的\nearned（挣得）填入意为\"资本家挣得每一分剩余\"，把批判性的\"压榨\"说成了中性的\"赚取\"，且\nearn 不接 from the working class；本句是批判资本家的观点句，需要带负面色彩的词，\nearned 色彩不符、搭配不通，双重出局。\nI) increased 搭配不成立：increase 不接 from，且方向反\nincreased（增加）是经济类文章高频词，看见 surplus 容易顺手选它——这是本空最大的坑。\n但 increase 从不与 from the working class 搭配；且\"资本家增加剩余\"是说资本家变富，与本\n句\"工人被压榨所以生活水平下降\"的论证方向相反。它真正的位置在 28 题。\nK) occurred 不及物：带不动宾语 surplus\noccurred（发生）是不及物动词，后面不能接宾语 every ounce of surplus；\"资本家发生了剩\n余\"语法、语义双不通。它真正的位置在 29 题（this improvement occurred）。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— increased 是经济文高频词，见 surplus 易顺手选。\nincreased（增加）词性对、话题贴，\"资本家增加每一分剩余\"初读也顺——但它不接 from\nthe working class，且本句是\"认为生活水平下降\"一方的控诉（Those that believed they\nfell claimed that...），需要负面词。防坑口诀：先定本句褒贬，再查空后介词——from 锁\n定 squeeze...from 搭配，squeezed 才是答案，increased 的真正归宿是 28 题。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) available\n形容词\n【词性槽】with+名词+补足语结构，空后是介词 to → 形容词\n【依据】available to sb. 固定搭配，且呼应 consumer-friendly\navailable 填入得 more and more goods available to the working class（越来越多商品\n对工人阶级\"可得\"）——available to sb. 是四级核心搭配。句内更有铁证：前面说 the\nenvironment was consumer-friendly，\"消费环境友好\"正是\"商品越来越买得到\"，语义完\n全咬合。\n【竞争词】B) deteriorating 逻辑指向反：与 consumer-friendly 矛盾\ndeteriorating（恶化的）若填入，意为\"越来越多恶化的商品流向工人\"，与本句 consumer-\nfriendly（对消费者友好）的正面语境直接冲突；且 deteriorate 是不及物义， deteriorating to\nthe working class 搭配也不成立。\nG) exhausting 搭配不当：exhausting 不接 to，也不修饰 goods\nexhausting（令人筋疲力尽的）修饰的是过程、工作、行程一类事物，说\"令人疲惫的商品\"不\n通；它也没有 exhausting to sb. 的用法，空后的 to the working class 无法安顿。\nL) peculiar 无中生有：全文无\"奇怪\"语义支撑\npeculiar（奇怪的）填入意为\"越来越多奇怪的商品\"，文章通篇讨论生活水平升降，没有任何语\n境支持\"奇怪\"这一评价；它属于命题人放的纯凑数形容词，靠\"词性对\"诱选。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) increased\n动词\n【词性槽】宾语从句主语 the standards of living 后缺谓语 → 动词\n【依据】increased 呼应下句 this improvement，定调\"总体上升\"\nincreased 填入得 overall, the standards of living increased（总体上生活水平上升了）。\n铁证在下一句：They do not agree, however, on when this improvement __(29)__.——\nthis improvement（这一改善）只能回指\"上升\"，若生活水平没升，improvement 一词无\n从谈起。overall 也与首段论战whether the standards of living rose or fell呼应：当代史\n学家给出的结论是 rose 一方。\n【竞争词】C) earned 及物动词无宾语：\"生活水平挣得\"不通\nearned（挣得）是及物动词，必须带宾语（earn money），本空后无任何宾语；且\"生活水\n平\"不能发出\"挣\"的动作。earned 的真正位置在 32 题 money earned。\nK) occurred 语义不搭配：\"发生\"的是改善，不是生活水平\noccurred（发生）填入意为\"生活水平发生了\"，occurred 的主语应是事件（improvement、\nchange），\"水平\"无所谓发生；且下句 this improvement 表明本句已交代\"上升\"这一事实，\noccurred 无法提供这个前提。occurred 的真正位置在 29 题。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) occurred\n动词\n【词性槽】从句主语 this improvement 后缺谓语 → 动词（过去式）\n【依据】improvement occurred：改善\"发生\"，与 when 天然搭配\noccurred 填入得 when this improvement occurred（这一改善何时发生）——occur\n（发生）的主语正是事件类名词，improvement occurred 是自然搭配；且本句讨论的就是\n时间分歧（史学家 agree 总体上升了，但不 agree \"何时\"），\"何时发生\"语义满分。\n【竞争词】C) earned 及物动词无宾语，且\"改善被挣得\"语义不通\nearned 必须带宾语（earn sth.），空后什么都没有；即便理解成被动\"改善被挣得\"也不符合英\n语表达习惯。when 引导的时间疑问与\"挣得\"毫无语义联系，occurred 才是\"何时\"问题的标准\n动词。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) exactly\n副词\n【词性槽】动词 define 与宾语从句之间 → 副词修饰 define\n【依据】define exactly：精确界定，呼应下句\"幸福无法测量\"\nexactly 填入得 define exactly what \"standard of living\" means（精确定义\"生活水平\"的\n含义）。下一句立刻给出理由：They all seem to agree that it should mean happiness,\nbut happiness is impossible to measure.——正因为概念模糊难测，史学家才要努力把它\n定义\"精确\"，exactly 与这段的语义走向严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】M) probably 语义冲突：定义是确定性动作，不能\"大概地定义\"\nprobably（大概、可能）表不确定，而 define（下定义）本身就是为了消除不确定——\"试图大\n概地定义\"自相矛盾。本句语气是史学家主动的学术努力，不是猜测。\nO) typically 方向不合：此处讲定义的精确度，不讲惯例\ntypically（通常地）强调\"按惯例\"，而本句焦点是 define 的程度（定义得多精确），\"通常地定\n义\"答非所问。typically 真正的用武之地在下一空（31），它描述的是\"史学家通常采用什么指\n标\"。两个副词空相邻，正是命题人故意设置的近位干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) typically\n副词\n【词性槽】主语 historians 与谓语 use 之间 → 副词\n【依据】typically：通常采用，陈述学界的惯例做法\ntypically 填入得 historians typically use \"real income\" as the measure（史学家通常\n以\"实际收入\"为衡量标准）。句首 Instead 承接上句\"幸福无法测量\"：既然幸福测不了，史\n学界的惯常做法就是退而用实际收入——typically 描述的正是这种通行惯例，与上文 They\nall seem to agree 的\"共识\"语气一致。\n【竞争词】M) probably 语气不合：作者在陈述事实，非推测\nprobably（大概、可能）填入意为\"史学家可能使用实际收入\"，但本段是作者对史学界的客观陈\n述（前有 They all seem to agree，后有具体的 real income 定义展开），不是作者的猜测；用\nprobably 会把确定事实降格为推测，与行文语气矛盾。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) earned\n动词\n【词性槽】money 后与 adjusted 并列 → 过去分词作后置定语\n【依据】money earned：挣到的钱，正合 real income 的定义\nearned 填入得 money earned and then adjusted according to the cost of living（挣\n到的钱，再按生活成本加以调整）——这正是\"实际收入\"的标准定义：名义收入（你挣的\n钱）剔除物价因素。earned 作后置定语修饰 money，语法语义双通。即便不认得 real\nincome 这个术语，靠\"并列形态+词库只剩它\"也能锁定。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) exhausting\n形容词\n【词性槽】介词 After 后、名词 debate 前 → 形容词作定语\n【依据】exhausting debate：旷日持久、令人疲惫的争论，呼应首段\"最古老之争\"\nexhausting 填入得 After exhausting debate and analysis（在令人筋疲力尽的辩论与分\n析之后）——本词的最大佐证在全文开头：The oldest debates were over whether the\nstandards of living rose or fell. 这场争论从工业革命延续至今，称得上\"旷日持久、令人疲\n惫\"；且本句结果是 historians have agreed to disagree（只好同意\"保留分歧\"），正是争\n到疲惫仍无定论的写照。\n【竞争词】B) deteriorating 搭配不当：deteriorate 指情况变坏，不修饰 debate\ndeteriorating（不断恶化的）描述的是局势、健康、关系的变坏，\"恶化的辩论\"不是英语的自然\n搭配；考生易把\"争论越来越久\"误译为\"争论恶化\"，这是母语思维代入了——文章只说争论持\n久，没说争论变质。\nL) peculiar 无中生有：无任何语境支持\"奇怪的辩论\"\npeculiar（奇怪的）填入意为\"在奇怪的辩论之后\"，全文对这场争论的描述是\"大、老、难\"，唯\n独没有\"奇怪\"。它是纯靠词性对来凑数的干扰项，识破靠一句：文中有没有对应语义支撑？没有\n就不选。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— 把\"争论旷日持久\"母语翻译成\"争论恶化\"。考生读到史学家\n争了两百年，容易用中文\"愈演愈烈\"去套 deteriorating（恶化的）。但 deteriorate 在英语\n里惯常修饰 situation/health/relationship，指\"质量变坏\"，不修饰 debate；文章也只说\n争论\"古老、重大\"，从不说它\"变质\"。防坑口诀：拿不准的形容词，问\"英语里它常和谁搭\n配\"——debate 常与 exhausting/heated 同行，不与 deteriorating 同行，exhausting\n才对。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) interpret\n动词\n【词性槽】decide to 后接原形动词，且空后带 how 宾语从句 → 及物动词原形\n【依据】interpret + how 从句，顺接\"史学家的研究动作\"\ninterpret 意为\"解读、解释\"，interpret how the Industrial Revolution was affected by\nother events 即\"解读工业革命如何受其他事件影响\"。上文刚说史学家对经济增长快慢\nhistorians have agreed to disagree，Instead 一转：他们转而决定去\"解读\"另一个问题\n——主语是历史学家，动作是研究性动作，interpret 完全贴合。\n【竞争词】E) enhance 搭配不成立：enhance 只接名词宾语\nenhance（增强、提升）是及物动词但只能接名词性宾语（enhance the quality），不能接\nhow 引导的从句；且\"历史学家增强工业革命如何被影响\"语义荒谬——史学家研究历史，不改变\n历史。\nH) experience 语义不通：今人无法\"体验\"历史事件\nexperience 作动词意为\"亲身经历、体验\"，历史学家不可能亲身\"体验\"两百年前工业革命如何被\n其他事件影响；experience 也不接 how 从句作学术讨论的对象。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) available", "B) deteriorating", "C) earned", "D) effect", "E) enhance", "F) exactly", "G) exhausting", "H) experience", "I) increased", "J) interpret", "K) occurred", "L) peculiar", "M) probably", "N) squeezed", "O) typically"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) effect\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 positive 后、had 的宾语位 → 名词\n【依据】positive effect 是固定搭配，且呼应下文 neutralized\neffect 填入得 an overall positive effect（总体上的积极影响），是英语里最常见的评价框\n架；更关键的是后半句 but it quite possibly was neutralized by negative events such\nas wars, high taxes, and rapid population growth——\"积极影响被负面事件抵消\"，\neffect 与 neutralized 形成自然的因果呼应。\n【竞争词】H) experience 主客体错位：经历属于人，影响才属于事件\nexperience 作\"经历、体验\"讲时，其主体必须是有感受的人（如 the workers' experience）；\n本句主语是 the Industrial Revolution 这一历史事件，历史事件对人的生活产生的是\"影响\"而\n非\"体验\"。且 experience 无法被下文 negative events neutralized 呼应，逻辑链断裂。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "The author's daughter slept throughout the last leg of their flight to Great Barrier Island.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】三岁的女儿在航班最后一段睡着\n【定位】Whether it was the gravity force of the plane climbing or the noise of the engine, it put\nour three-year-old daughter instantly to sleep until we bumped down on the grass at\nClaris Airport.\n无论是飞机爬升时的重力还是引擎的轰鸣，都让我们三岁的女儿立刻入睡，直到我们在克拉\n里斯机场的草地上颠簸着陆。\n【改写】The author's daughter slept throughout the last leg of their flight to Great Barrier Island ↔\nour three-year-old daughter instantly to sleep until we bumped down on the grass at Claris\nAirport\n同义替换与语义压缩", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The lodge where the author stayed provided very delicious food.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】旅馆提供美味食物\n【定位】Teara used to be a chef at the Langham in Auckland and so dinner, eaten around a huge\ncircular table (made of one of the most ancient trees in the world), was every bit as mouth-\nwatering as you'd expect.\nTeara 曾在奥克兰的 Langham 酒店担任主厨，因此围坐在一张由世界上最古老的树木之一\n制成的巨大圆桌旁的晚餐，果然令人垂涎欲滴。\n【改写】The lodge where the author stayed ↔ Mount St Paul's Estate, a luxurious lodge\n同义替换/具体化\nprovided very delicious food ↔ dinner ... was every bit as mouth-watering as you'd expect\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 A A 段有 our lodge balcony，但只讲看星空，没有 delicious food 相关描述。\nour lodge balcony", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Many people are attracted to Great Barrier Island because of the incredible fishing sites around it.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】钓鱼点吸引许多人\n【定位】Lots of people come here for the fantastic fishing spots around the island.\n很多人来到这里是冲着岛上绝佳的钓鱼点。\n【改写】Many people are attracted to Great Barrier Island ↔ Lots of people come here\n同义替换\nbecause of the incredible fishing sites around it ↔\nfor the fantastic fishing spots around the island\n同义替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "A well-known artist failed to purchase a house on Great Barrier Island.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】知名艺术家买房被拒\n【定位】Apparently the island council turned down famous musician Paul McCartney's application\nto buy a property at Great Barrier Island because of the unwelcome attention he would\nhave brought.\n据报道，岛议会因担心他会带来不必要的关注，拒绝了著名音乐人 Paul McCartney 在大屏\n障岛购买房产的申请。\n【改写】A well-known artist ↔ famous musician Paul McCartney\n概括/同义\nfailed to purchase a house ↔ turned down ... application to buy a property\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 F F 段提到 commune of artists，但讲的是 Waiheke Island 的艺术家聚居，未涉及\nPaul McCartney 或买房被拒。\ncommune of artists", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Unable to fall asleep after a long flight, the author was pleased to enjoy the most gorgeous view of the night sky in his life.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】失眠后看到最美夜空\n【定位】Wide awake at 3:30 am with our nine-year-old daughter on our lodge balcony, I stared for\nhours at the most incredible night sky I've ever seen before the dawn slowly started to rub out\nthe stars on the horizon and the multiple shooting stars faded away.\n凌晨3:30，我带着九岁的女儿在旅馆阳台上完全清醒，凝视了数小时我所见过的最不可思议\n的夜空，直到黎明慢慢抹去地平线上的星星，众多流星也渐渐消逝。\n【改写】Unable to fall asleep after a long flight ↔ terrible jetlag. Wide awake at 3:30 am\n同义替换/因果\nthe most gorgeous view of the night sky in his life ↔ the most incredible night sky I've ever seen\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 B B 段有 exceptional or distinguished quality of starry nights，但说的是岛屿被授予\n称号，并非作者个人在凌晨看到的夜空。\nexceptional or distinguished quality of starry nights", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Great Barrier Island is compared to another island which attracted a lot of artists and people who wished to stay away from city life half a century ago.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】与半世纪前吸引艺术家的另一岛屿相比\n【定位】The locals are a relaxed and friendly bunch—a lot of them told us that it's like what Waiheke\nIsland (now in effect a suburb of Auckland) was in the 1970s when it was a commune of\nartists and people wanting to escape the city.\n当地人是一群放松又友好的人——他们中很多人告诉我们，这里就像怀赫科岛（现在实际上\n是奥克兰的郊区）在1970年代的样子，当时它是艺术家和想要逃离城市的人的公社。\n【改写】Great Barrier Island is compared to another island ↔ it's like what Waiheke Island ... was\n同义替换\nwhich attracted a lot of artists ↔ commune of artists\n同义替换\npeople who wished to stay away from city life ↔ people wanting to escape the city\n同义替换\nhalf a century ago ↔ in the 1970s\n时间对应\n【辨邻】 K K 段有 potter(陶艺家) Sarah Harrison，属于艺术家个体，但未把大屏障岛与另一岛\n作比较。\npotter(陶艺家) Sarah Harrison", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "One most memorable part of the author's visit to Great Barrier Island was a one-hour walk to its centre's hot springs.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】一小时的温泉步行\n【定位】Another highlight was an hour-long walk to Kaitoke hot pools in the centre of the island,\nthrough wetlands and a forest, where we whiled away a good hour sitting in the pools shaded\nfrom the heat by layers of delicate umbrella-like plants.\n另一个亮点是步行一小时前往岛中部的 Kaitoke 温泉，穿越湿地和森林，我们在温泉里消磨\n了整整一个小时，头顶是层层 delicate 伞状植物遮挡烈日。\n【改写】One most memorable part ↔ Another highlight\n同义替换\na one-hour walk to its centre's hot springs ↔\nan hour-long walk to Kaitoke hot pools in the centre of the island\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 E E 段提到 good walking tracks，但讲的是山间步道与户外活动，未涉及 Kaitoke\nhot pools。\ngood walking tracks", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Great Barrier Island has officially been recognized for its extraordinary starry nights.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】官方认可其非凡星空\n【定位】Great Barrier Island, just off the coast, had been recently awarded Dark Sky Sanctuary status\n—a typically remote area that has an exceptional or distinguished quality of starry nights—\nwe thought it would be the perfect place to relax before our busy schedule.\n距海岸不远的大屏障岛最近被授予“暗夜天空保护区”称号——这类偏远地区拥有“卓越或杰出\n的星空质量”——我们认为这是在繁忙行程前放松的绝佳去处。\n【改写】officially been recognized ↔ had been recently awarded Dark Sky Sanctuary status\n同义替换\nits extraordinary starry nights ↔ exceptional or distinguished quality of starry nights\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 G G 段有 explaining how the island was awarded its special status，但说的是光污\n染测量，不是“官方授予 Dark Sky Sanctuary status”这一结果本身。\nexplaining how the island was awarded its special status", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Great Barrier Island is not connected to any public power supply lines.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】未接入公共电网\n【定位】Great Barrier Island has no public facilities and the 950 people living there (though only\nabout half permanently) rely on solar and wind energy, and bottled gas for cooking.\n大屏障岛没有公共设施，居住在那里的950人（虽然只有大约一半长期居住）依靠太阳能和\n风能，以及瓶装燃气做饭。\n【改写】not connected to any public power supply lines ↔ has no public facilities\n同义替换/概括\nnot connected to any public power supply lines ↔\nrely on solar and wind energy, and bottled gas for cooking\n具体说明能源来源", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "It is strange that light pollution on Great Barrier Island mainly comes from a faraway island to the north.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】光污染主要来自北方远岛\n【定位】they found there was more light pollution on the northern side coming from Fiji, which is\nabout 3,000km away, than Auckland to the south.\n他们发现，来自约3000公里外斐济的光污染在北部更多，超过了南部的奥克兰。\n【改写】It is strange ↔ It's odd\n同义替换\nlight pollution on Great Barrier Island mainly comes from a faraway island to the north ↔\nmore light pollution on the northern side coming from Fiji, which is about 3,000km away, than\nAuckland to the south\n同义替换\n【辨邻】 B B 段有 exceptional or distinguished quality of starry nights，但丝毫没有提到 Fiji\n或 light pollution 的来源。\nexceptional or distinguished quality of starry nights", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What is the classic grocery store dilemma?", "options": ["A) Whether or not one should eat organic food.", "B) Whether or not one can stretch their wallet far.", "C) Organic food costs more but it certainly tastes better.", "D) One wants the best food but their budget is limited."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the classic grocery store dilemma? 没有 infer/suggest/imply 等推断标记，而\n是直接询问文中某一表述指什么。\n【拆句】What is the classic grocery store dilemma? 是一个简单的主系表疑问句，主干为 What\nis the dilemma，无否定、比较或从句陷阱。\n【定位】On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your\nwallet can only stretch so far.\n一方面，你家餐桌上的人只配吃最好的；另一方面，你的钱包只能撑到这么多。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nA 项说'是否应该吃有机食品'，这是文章讨论的大话题，而非 the classic grocery store\ndilemma 所指的具体两难处境。\nB) 以偏概全\nB 项只提到'钱包能撑多远'，把原文预算受限的一面单独拿出来，却丢掉了'想要最好的食\n物'这一面，不能完整解释 dilemma。\nC) 无中生有\nC 项说有机食品'当然更好吃'，但原文 P1 只提到' deserves only the best'，并未说明\nbest 指味道，更无 certainly tastes better 的依据。\nD) ✓ 正确\nyour family dinner table deserves only the best ↔ One wants the best food\nyour wallet can only stretch so far ↔ their budget is limited\nD 项把 On the one hand ... On the other hand ... 的两侧都概括进去，既提到'想要最\n好的食物'，也提到'预算有限'。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn about organic food from science?", "options": ["A) Whether it is any better remains uncertain.", "B) Whether it is healthier is under consideration.", "C) Whether it is more nutritious than conventional food is arguable.", "D) Whether it is going to replace conventional food is still unclear."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】What do we learn about organic food from science? 用 learn 提问，表面像细节，实则\n要求把科学观点概括后推出。\n【拆句】What do we learn about organic food from science? 主干为 What do we learn about\nA from B，about 引出对象，from 引出信息来源，无复杂从句。\n【定位】Science won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is 'better'.\n科学不会对'有机食品是否更好'这个问题给出一个明确的答案。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nScience won't give a clear-cut answer ↔ Whether it is any better remains uncertain.\nwhether organic food is 'better'] ↔ Whether it is any better\nA 项 'Whether it is any better remains uncertain' 准确概括了 Science won't give a\nclear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is 'better'. 的核心含义。\nB) 偷换概念\nB 项说'是否更健康仍在考虑中'，但原文说的是科学没有 clear-cut answer，而不是该问\n题'正在考虑'。\nC) 与原文相反\nC 项说'是否更有营养还可争论'，但 Organic and conventionally grown food are\nequally nutritious. 已明确给出结论：两者营养相同，不存在可争论空间。\nD) 无中生有\nD 项说'是否会取代传统食品仍不清楚'，但全文没有讨论 organic food 取代\nconventional food 的问题，属于无中生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the passage say about organic and non-organic products as far as food safety is concerned?", "options": ["A) Their difference is the conventions used to test them.", "B) They differ in how much pesticide remains on them.", "C) One relies on pesticides whereas the other does not.", "D) There is no substantial difference between the two."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about organic and non-organic products as far as food\nsafety is concerned? 要求找文章对某具体问题的明确说法。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about organic and non-organic products as far as food\nsafety is concerned? 中 as far as ... is concerned 是固定搭配，意为'就……而言'，把讨论\n范围限定在食品安全。\n【定位】There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-\nsafety perspective.\n从食品安全的角度来看，有机产品和非有机产品之间也没有很大差别。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nA 项说'差异在于测试它们的惯例'，但原文 P5 没有任何关于 testing conventions 的讨\n论，conventional 与 conventions 被强行混淆。\nB) 与原文相反\nB 项说'两者在农药残留量上有差异'，但 Testing shows little if any pesticide remains\non conventionally grown products. 表明传统种植产品上的农药残留极少，且总体结\n论是没有 big difference。\nC) 与原文相反\nC 项说'一种用农药，另一种不用'，但 Even organic growers use pesticides. 明确说明\n有机种植者也使用农药。\nD) ✓ 正确\nno big difference ↔ no substantial difference\nfrom a food-safety perspective ↔ as far as food safety is concerned\nD 项 'There is no substantial difference between the two' 与 There is also no big\ndifference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety\nperspective. 几乎逐词对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author say about organic farming?", "options": ["A) It may be blamed for the shrinking bee populations in the world.", "B) It minimizes the use of controversial pesticides in its operations.", "C) It may impact the environment less than conventional farming.", "D) It contaminates groundwater as much as conventional farming."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about organic farming? 问的是作者对有机农业的具体看法，\n可在文中找到直接陈述。\n【拆句】What does the author say about organic farming? 为简单疑问句，about organic\nfarming 限定话题范围，无否定或比较结构。\n【定位】On the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint.\n总体而言，有机农业可能对环境的影响更小。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nA 项说'有机农业可能因全球蜜蜂数量减少而受指责'，但原文 Organic farmers never\nuse neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee\npopulations worldwide. 说的是有机农业不用导致蜜蜂减少的农药，反而应是被肯\n定的。\nB) 以偏概全\nB 项说'它在运营中最小化使用有争议的农药'，但原文只提到有机农民 'never use\nneonics'，并未说'最小化使用所有争议农药'，且 P5-P6 还指出有机农业使用更多天然\n农药。\nC) ✓ 正确\norganic agriculture ↔ organic farming\nhave a smaller environmental footprint ↔\nimpact the environment less than conventional farming\nC 项 'It may impact the environment less than conventional farming' 与 On the\nwhole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. 语义完全\n对应。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 项说'它污染地下水程度与传统农业相当'，但 the risk of groundwater\ncontamination from organic farming is lower 明确说有机农业地下水污染风险更低。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do advocates of organic farming argue about its yields and efficiency?", "options": ["A) They would increase quickly with the emergence of new technologies.", "B) They would improve remarkably with more research and investment.", "C) They would be achieved along with enormous health benefits.", "D) They would considerably lower the cost to the environment."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do advocates of organic farming argue about its yields and efficiency? 问支持\n者对某具体问题的论点。\n【拆句】What do advocates of organic farming argue about its yields and efficiency? 主干为\nWhat do advocates argue about A，advocates of organic farming 是动作主体，its\nyields and efficiency 是讨论对象。\n【定位】On the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be\nsignificantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic\nfarming as it does conventional agriculture.\n另一方面，有机农业的支持者认为，如果食品行业像投入传统农业那样把同样多的研究和资\n金投入到有机农业上，产量和效率可能会显著提高。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nA 项说'随着新技术出现，产量和效率会快速提升'，但原文条件为 'research and\nmoney'，并非 new technologies。\nB) ✓ 正确\ndevoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture ↔\nwith more research and investment\nsignificantly improved ↔ improve remarkably\nB 项 'They would improve remarkably with more research and investment' 准确对\n应 yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if ... devoted as much\nresearch and money ... 的条件与结果。\nC) 无中生有\nC 项说'产量和效率会伴随着巨大健康收益而实现'，但原文支持者只讨论 yields and\nefficiency，未提及 health benefits。\nD) 答非所问\nD 项说'它们会大幅降低环境成本'，但题干问的是 advocates 对 yields and efficiency\n的论点，而非环境影响。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is the finding of one recent study about being selfish?", "options": ["A) It may bring certain benefits in the short term.", "B) It usually yields more disadvantages than advantages.", "C) It serves individual interest rather than a collective's.", "D) It may give way to cooperating in the long run."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干What is the finding of ...?直接询问某研究的客观结论，属于对文中具体信息的查找。\n【拆句】题干What is the finding of one recent study about being selfish?是主系表特殊疑问\n句：What（表语）+ is（系动词）+ the finding of one recent study about being\nselfish（主语），整体询问关于 being selfish 的研究发现。\n【定位】One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater\ngood. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may\nbe short-lived, however.\n一项近期研究测试了人们是否愿意搁置自私利益以实现更大的善。研究发现，自私比合作更\n有利。然而，这种好处可能是短暂的。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nbeing selfish was more advantageous than cooperating ↔ bring certain benefits\nThe benefit may be short-lived, however. ↔ in the short term\nIt may bring certain benefits in the short term.把原文的'more\nadvantageous'和'short-lived'分别同义替换为'bring certain benefits'和'in the short\nterm'，且保留了may的委婉语气。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt usually yields more disadvantages than advantages.与原文'being selfish was\nmore advantageous than cooperating'直接矛盾。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt serves individual interest rather than a collective's.把实验设计中的'selfish\ninterests'和'greater good'篡改为研究结论，但原文并未说研究发现自私服务于个人而\n非集体。\nD) 张冠李戴\nIt may give way to cooperating in the long run.借用了原文'in the long run'，但\n把'players who cooperated did better in the long run'说成'selfish may give way to\ncooperating'，改变了主语与语义。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What have researchers found by studying identical and non-identical twins?", "options": ["A) They are overwhelmingly different in the amounts of genes.", "B) One's behavior is largely determined by their genetic traits.", "C) One's biological roots can be identified by their genes.", "D) They differ from each other in their behavior patterns."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干What have researchers found ...?询问研究人员的具体发现，属于细节题。\n【拆句】题干What have researchers found by studying identical and non-identical twins?是\nwhat引导的特殊疑问句，主语researchers，谓语have found，by studying...twins为方式\n状语，询问通过双胞胎研究得到的发现。\n【定位】By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical\ntwins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic\neffects on behaviors.\n通过比较基因几乎100%相同的同卵双胞胎和约一半基因相同的异卵双胞胎，研究人员发现\n了行为受基因影响的压倒性证据。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThey are overwhelmingly different in the amounts of genes.将原文对双胞胎基因共\n享比例的客观描述改写成研究结论，且改变了比较对象。\nB) ✓ 正确\ngenetic effects on behaviors ↔ behavior is largely determined by their genetic traits\noverwhelming evidence ↔ largely determined\nOne's behavior is largely determined by their genetic traits.是'overwhelming\nevidence for genetic effects on behaviors'的同义概\n括，'largely'与'overwhelming'在程度上对应。\nC) 偷换概念\nOne's biological roots can be identified by their genes.中的'biological roots'来自\n本段背景句，而'can be identified'夸大了研究进展，不是双胞胎研究的直接发现。\nD) 无中生有\nThey differ from each other in their behavior patterns.在原文没有对应信息，原文关\n注的是基因对行为的影响，而非双胞胎行为模式之间的差异。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What did the author and colleagues identify in their recent research?", "options": ["A) A gene-related selfish behavior in preschool children.", "B) Key factors that determine one's altruistic behavior.", "C) A specific gene associated with altruistic behavior.", "D) Roles played by genes in shaping one's character."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干What did the author and colleagues identify ...?询问具体识别出的内容，属于细\n节题。\n【拆句】题干What did the author and colleagues identify in their recent research?是what引导\n的特殊疑问句，主语the author and colleagues，谓语did...identify，in their recent\nresearch为范围状语，询问近期研究中识别出的对象。\n【定位】My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic(利他的) behavior and\nfound that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\n我和我的同事最近识别出一种与利他行为相关的基因，并发现它的某种变体与学龄前儿童的\n自私行为有关。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nA gene-related selfish behavior in preschool children.只截取了定位句后半句，忽略\n了作者首先'identified a gene linked to altruistic behavior'这一核心对象。\nB) 过度推断\nKey factors that determine one's altruistic behavior.用复数'Key\nfactors'和'determine'，把原文仅识别的一个基因及其关联上升为决定利他行为的多个\n关键因素，过度推断。\nC) ✓ 正确\nidentified a gene linked to altruistic(利他的) behavior ↔\nA specific gene associated with altruistic behavior.\na gene ↔ A specific gene\nA specific gene associated with altruistic behavior.将'identified'转换\n为'associated'，'a gene'具体化为'a specific gene'，其余成分一致。\nD) 过度推断\nRoles played by genes in shaping one's character.用'shaping one's character'替换\n了原文'altruistic behavior'，并把范围扩大到'genes in shaping character'，属于上位\n的过度泛化。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do we learn from evolutionary scientists about cooperative behavior?", "options": ["A) It may have initially originated in individuals linked by blood.", "B) It may have caused the diversification of communities.", "C) It helps to build relationships among blood-related people.", "D) It guarantees the achievement of goals otherwise unattainable."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干What do we learn from evolutionary scientists about ...?要求获取进化科学家的具\n体观点，属于细节题。\n【拆句】题干What do we learn from evolutionary scientists about cooperative behavior?是\nwhat引导的特殊疑问句，'do we learn from evolutionary scientists'为谓语部分，'about\ncooperative behavior'限定讨论对象。\n【定位】According to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among\nrelatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line.\n根据进化科学家的观点，合作行为可能首先在亲属之间演化，以促进其基因谱系的延续。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives ↔\nIt may have initially originated in individuals linked by blood.\nmay have evolved ↔ may have initially originated\nIt may have initially originated in individuals linked by blood.将'relatives'替换\n为'individuals linked by blood'，将'evolved first'替换为'initially originated'，且保\n留了'may'。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt may have caused the diversification of communities.将原文'As communities\ndiversified, mutual support could have broadened'中的时间伴随关系改写成因果\n关系。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt helps to build relationships among blood-related people.把原文'promote the\ncontinuation of their genetic line'改写为'build relationships among blood-related\npeople'，从'基因延续'变为'建立关系'，改变了目的。\nD) 过度推断\nIt guarantees the achievement of goals otherwise unattainable.用'guarantees'替\n换了原文'higher chances'和'can work together'中的可能性，属于程度上的过度\n推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What can we infer from the last paragraph?", "options": ["A) Cooperative behavior is evident among identical twins.", "B) One is born either cooperative or non-cooperative.", "C) Genes determine one's tendency to cooperate.", "D) Cooperative behavior may be cultivated."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题）\n【判型】题干What can we infer from the last paragraph?中的infer表明需要从末段信息推导出言\n外之意。\n【拆句】题干What can we infer from the last paragraph?是what引导的特殊疑问句，'can we\ninfer'表示推断，'from the last paragraph'限定推断范围。\n【定位】Yet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling\nand parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act\ncooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and\nenvironmental influences.\n然而，几乎没有行为是完全由基因决定的，即使在同卵双胞胎中也是如此。文化、学校教育\n以及养育方式是合作的重要决定因素。因此，我们表现出合作或自私的程度因人而异，并取\n决于多种基因和环境影响。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nCooperative behavior is evident among identical twins.在末段没有对应信息，原文\n只说'almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins'，并未讨\n论同卵双胞胎中合作行为是否明显。\nB) 与原文相反\nOne is born either cooperative or non-cooperative.用'either...or'表达先天固定二\n分，与原文'depends on a variety of genetic and environmental\ninfluences'及'unique to each individual'相矛盾。\nC) 与原文相反\nGenes determine one's tendency to cooperate.只看见'genetic'却忽略了'not\nentirely genetic'和'environmental influences'，属于抓半句的相反理解。\nD) ✓ 正确\nCulture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. ↔\nCooperative behavior may be cultivated.\ndepends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences ↔ may be cultivated\nCooperative behavior may be cultivated.把'Culture, schooling and parenting are\nimportant determinants'概括为'may be cultivated'，没有超出原文范围。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
